// error
// #1535: E0052/E0072 for type annotations that sit OUTSIDE a function
// declaration. `vTypeNodes` had exactly one non-recursive call site — vFuncDecl
// — so a type node reached by neither the statement walk (function bodies, via
// vStmt since #1521) nor a signature sweep was never judged at all. Module-level
// `let`/`const` annotations and class/alias/enum member types are that gap:
// the self-hosted compiler accepted every declaration below in silence.
//
// A silent ACCEPTANCE, which is the class this project keeps paying for —
// #1522 (the arrow subtree was skipped entirely) and #1529 (calls through a
// function value never checked their arguments) were both the same shape.
//
// ORDER IS THE POINT, and it is not source order. Measured against the seed
// (the oracle) rather than assumed, because #1489 established that guessing the
// traversal produces a confidently wrong fix. The seed checks every type
// DECLARATION ahead of every module BINDING, so `c` is declared first here and
// is nevertheless reported last. Within a binding the sequence is the seed's
// checkBinding: the annotation's own type nodes, then the initializer's, then
// the assignability verdict — post-order throughout, per SPEC.md §14.8.
//
// `c`'s initializer is deliberately a CONSTANT: a non-constant one raises E0064
// too, which is #1534's gap, and mixing them would leave neither case pinning
// its own fix.
//
// Single-file golden, so there is NO prelude: `print` takes a string.

const c: [2][]int = 0

class S {
  a: [2][]int,
  b: map<[]int, int>,
}

type Al = map<[]int, int>

enum E {
  V(map<[]int, int>),
}

fn main() {
  print("x\n")
}
